        "narrative_words": 260,
        "narrative": "The account received a credit of Rs.21,50,000 on 2026-09-16 via SWIFT from Gulf Logistics LLC, Abu Dhabi, UAE, which is inconsistent with the customer's declared profile as a returned resident with a monthly income of Rs.25,000 from pension and rent. This transaction exceeds the customer's declared income, triggering POE-005 (Income Level). The subsequent debit of Rs.20,00,000 on 2026-09-21 to a term deposit in the same name and bank raises concerns, as it may indicate a pass-through pattern, triggering POE-010 (Transaction Complexity).\n\nThe large credit and subsequent term deposit also trigger POE-002 (Business Profile) and POE-009 (Transaction Size), respectively. The transaction's origin from Abu Dhabi, UAE, triggers POE-011 (Geographies). Furthermore, the customer's returned resident status and the high-value transaction trigger POE-004 (Customer Risk Profile). The end-of-service payment without documents triggers POE-006 (Source of Income).\n\nThe record does not rule out the possibility that the credit represents a legitimate end-of-service payment. However, it contradicts the customer's declared income, as the credit amount exceeds their monthly income by a significant margin. The term deposit transaction also contradicts the customer's declared business profile, as it does not align with their stated sources of income.\n\nThe pattern of transactions and the customer's profile trigger multiple red-flag indicators, including the RFI-006 alert signal. Pursuant to the Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002, Section 12(1)(b), a report is required to be made to the Financial Intelligence Unit - India, as the transactions may indicate a possible proceeds of crime. The transactions and customer profile are inconsistent, and the large credit and subsequent term deposit warrant further examination.",
